Subject: [PATCH] feat(glm5_next): add zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash

GLM-5.3-Flash is 45 layers, 34 Kimi Delta Attention and 11 sparse MLA with no
position encoding, hidden 4096, a 4-wide mHC residual, 288 routed experts
top-8 plus one shared, 320B parameters and 18B active. The checkpoint names
Glm5NextForConditionalGeneration with model_type glm5_next and ships the
attention, KDA, router and mHC weights in BF16 and the MLA projections, the
dense MLPs and every expert in FP8 E4M3 with a weight_scale_inv per 128 x 128
tile. This serves the text backbone. The vision tower and the MTP layer load
nothing.

The port follows transformers 5.17 models/glm5_next/modeling_glm5_next.py.

models/glm5_next.py:

- mHC: each sublayer collapses the four copies with its own pre mix, and post
  and a Sinkhorn-normalized comb expand its output and mix the residual in.
  The final hidden state is the mean of the copies, then the norm. Plain XLA,
  since kernels/mhc/tune.py schedules v6e alone.
- KDA: the per-channel decay with the lower bound,
  lower_bound * sigmoid(exp(A_log) * (f + dt_bias)), the width-4 causal
  convolutions on q, k and v, and the sigmoid-gated RMS norm on the output.
  The recurrence runs as the chunked form in plain XLA, 64 tokens a chunk,
  the grid cut at every request start, one lax.scan over the chunks.
- Sparse MLA: q and the 512-wide latent from the LoRA projections, the
  attention absorbed against the cached latent, heads split over the tensor
  axis in shard_map. The indexer pools each run of index_kpool keys with a
  per-channel softmax over gate scores plus a learned position term, scores
  the pools with ReLU dot products weighted per head, keeps the best
  index_topk // index_kpool of those whose last token the query sees, and
  adds the open tail. The per-head scores sum across shards with one psum.
- MoE: sigmoid routing with a selection-only bias, normalized top-k weights
  times routed_scaling_factor, EPMoE with its gmm step overridden for the
  SwiGLU clamp (gate from above, up both sides, at swiglu_limit), one shared
  expert.

State lives in mem_cache/glm5_next_state_pool.py, one row per request slot
plus a dummy row 0: the FP32 KDA state and convolution window of every KDA
layer, and per token the latent and the indexer's key and gate score of every
MLA layer. Every read is masked by the request's prefix length, so a slot
needs no reset. It holds no prefix, so the model refuses the radix cache.
model_runner_kv_cache_mixin.py gains the hooks a model class can define:
build_extra_memory_pools, paged_kv_layers and paged_kv_token_cap, so the
unused paged KV pool stays small.

configs/glm5_next.py registers Glm5NextServingConfig for glm5_next.
transformers ships Glm5NextConfig from 5.16 and sglang-jax pins 5.12. The
class flattens text_config, fills the defaults transformers fills, moves
quantization_config to checkpoint_quantization_config so the engine doesn't
serve FP8 linears, and moves layer_types aside from the sliding-window count.

The loader reads the safetensors headers itself, widens FP8 on the host, and
places every expert stack with make_array_from_callback, so a host
dequantizes only its own devices' experts. It raises on a checkpoint tensor
it neither reads nor skips, and on a parameter nothing wrote.

Capture: slot k of layers_to_capture holds the four copies entering layer k,
flattened to hc_mult * hidden_size values a token, which is transformers'
hidden_states[k] reshaped [tokens, 4 * 4096].
